WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:02.270
How do you choose a date?

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:04.050
Whose company would you enjoy?

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:07.180
Well one thing you can consider is looks.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:10.920
Like everything else these days courtship
has gone online.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.700
Can that so call science really find your
soul mate?

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:18.100
But I've never been out on a dinner date
before. I'm just scared to death.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.660
Online dating is now a multibillion-dollar
industry.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.440
One in five dating relationships started online.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.280
But is there really a formula for love?

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.120
It's 1959 and two electrical engineering
students at Stanford are working on their

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.059
final class project.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:46.100
It uses a questionnaire and an IBM 650 to
matchmake 49 men and 49 women.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.639
They call it the Happy Families Planning Service.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.460
There are no long-term matches but they do
get an A for their work.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.300
It was 1960s when computer scientists started
to get interested in personal relationships

00:00:58.309 --> 00:01:03.249
and in particular they were interested in
whether they could improve the efficiency

00:01:03.249 --> 00:01:05.260
by which people matched.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:10.980
Similar small data-driven systems appear but
in 1965 it's Operation Match that really

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.120
starts to 'take the blindness out of a blind
date'.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:19.070
For $3 you can answer over 100 questions about
yourself and your ideal date.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:24.220
The answers are recorded on punch cards, run
through a five-ton IBM computer and a few

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:29.320
weeks later, you get the names and numbers
of your top six matches.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.980
Computer technology has moved so fast.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:33.500
It matched on the basis of things that people

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:34.560
had in common.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.860
So religion, hobbies, attitudes
to life in general. And we actually know now

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:43.390
that that's not the most effective way of
matching people.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:48.000
Six months after the launch, 90,000 Operation
Match questionnaires are received,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.300
making the company $270,000.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.340
In 1995 Match.com is launched by American engineer Gary Kremen.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.190
And then in 2005 a group of employees

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:03.724
at PayPal create a website where people can

00:02:03.724 --> 00:02:07.340
upload videos of themselves to try and get a date.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.879
The tagline is Tune In, Hook Up.

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:15.280
It doesn't take off so instead it rebrands
itself as YouTube.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.960
Today Apple is going to reinvent the phone.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:23.740
It's 2007 and the iPhone has just been released.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:25.880
Soon new dating apps start to appear.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:31.180
Most dating apps are notoriously private about

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:34.880
the algorithms they use and that's because
they use quite simple ones.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.660
And they don't have much very much proof that any of them

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.200
lead to better matching.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.380
They don't want everyone to find love on the first hit

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.080
because then they'd all be out of business.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:48.080
In 2017 around 40% of American heterosexual
couples first meet online.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:54.569
And by 2018 dating services in the US are
estimated to be a $3 billion a year business.

00:02:54.569 --> 00:02:58.939
Lots of people anecdotally are saying they're
fed up of using dating apps but at the same time

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.560
business is absolutely booming.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:05.980
The number of users between 2016 and 2017 in the world doubled.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:12.100
Even though there's on the horizon, the possibility
of video dating and augmented reality for dating,

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:15.820
none of those things can give you
an indication of chemistry and you could only

00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:18.080
find that out when you meet somebody face
to face.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.359
That's still going to be overridingly

00:03:20.359 --> 00:03:22.279
the way in which people meet and fall in love.

